<USER_REQUEST>
A particle is projected upwards from the ground with an initial velocity of \( 20 \, \text{m/s} \). Assuming the acceleration due to gravity is \( 9.8 \, \text{m/s}^2 \) downward and neglecting air resistance, find the maximum height reached by the particle.
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
The maximum height \( h \) is the maximum distance the particle moves from the ground. At the maximum height, the velocity of the particle is zero. Using the kinematic equation:  
\[
v^2 = u^2 + 2as
\]  
where:  
- \( v = 0 \, \text{m/s} \) (final velocity at maximum height)  
- \( u = 20 \, \text{m/s} \) (initial velocity)  
- \( a = -9.8 \, \text{m/s}^2 \) (acceleration due to gravity, negative because it is downward)  
- \( s = h \) (distance, which is the maximum height)  

Substituting the values:  
\[
0 = 20^2 + 2(-9.8)h
\]  
\[
0 = 400 - 19.6h
\]  
\[
19.6h = 400
\]  
\[
h = \frac{400}{19.6} \approx 20.41 \, \text{m}
\]  

Thus, the maximum height reached by the particle is \(\boxed{20.41 \, \text{m}}\).
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

