The time \( t \) (in seconds) for a sample to travel a distance \( L \) (in cm) is given by:

\[
t = \frac{L}{v}
\]

where \( v \) is the flow velocity in cm s⁻¹.

First, we need to convert the flow rate to m³ min⁻¹:

\[
10 \text{ cm³ min}^{-1} = 10 \times 10^{-6} \text{ m³ min}^{-1} = \frac{10 \times 10^{-6}}{60} \text{ m³ s}^{-1} = 1.67 \times 10^{-7} \text{ m}^3 \text{ s}^{-1}
\]

Next, calculate the cross-sectional area of the tube:

\[
\text{Area} = \pi \left( \frac{0.1 \text{ mm}}{2} \right)^2 = \pi \left( 0.05 \text{ mm} \right)^2 = \pi \times 2.5 \times 10^{-6} \text{ m}^2 = 7.85 \times 10^{-6} \text{ m}^2
\]

Finally, calculate the flow velocity:

\[
v = \frac{\text{Flow Rate}}{\text{Area}} = \frac{1.67 \times 10^{-7} \text{ m}^3 \text{ s}^{-1}}{7.85 \times 10^{-6} \text{ m}^2} = 2.1 \times 10^{-2} \text{ m s}^{-1} = 2.1 \times 10^3 \text{ cm s}^{-1}
\]

Now, we can calculate the flow time for the given flow lengths:

1. For \( L = 1 \text{ cm} \):
   \[
   t = \frac{1 \text{ cm}}{2.1 \text{ cm s}^{-1}} = 0.47 \text{ s}
   \]

2. For \( L = 2 \text{ cm} \):
